{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Cojocaru2004-QWDW-N50-npa035",
  "Source": "E. Cojocaru, \"Quarter-wave discrete wavelet design of dichroic highly reflecting-transmitting mirrors for ultrafast solid-state lasers,\" Appl. Opt. 43, 1261-1265 (2004). doi:10.1364/AO.43.001261 - Sec. 3, Eqs. (1)-(3), pp. 1261-1262; parameters of Figs. 1, 2(b), 2(c) (solid) and 3 (solid): N = 50, na = 1.9, npa = 0.35 (npp = 0.7), lambda0 = 1 um, n0 = ns = na",
  "Notes": "Quarter-wave discrete wavelet (QWDW) high reflector for a dichroic ultrafast-laser mirror: 50 QW layers whose indices follow the upper/lower quintic envelopes na +/- npa A(x)\nSpecification: High-reflectance stop band centred at lambda0 = 1 um with no sidelobes; compared with a periodic QW stack of the same N, na, npa.\nReported: Fig. 3(a): full width of the stop band at R = 0.5 is almost the same as for the periodic QW stack (n1 = 2.25, n2 = 1.55, N = 50), given by Eq. (4): dl/l0 ~ (4/pi)(npa/na) = 0.2345 (Sec. 4: for N >= 40 the QWDW width at R = 0.5 differs from Eq. (4) by < 3 %). Fig. 3(b): QWDW peak R0 at lambda0 is lower than the periodic stack's (solid curve peaks a little below 1 on a 0.99-1 scale). Figs. 2(c)/3(a): no sidelobes outside the stop band. Fig. 4/Eq. (7) (f = 0.5164): R0 >= 0.9999 needs N ~ 55.5 at npa = 0.35, so N = 50 stays below 0.9999. Recomputed here at lambda0 with the assumed sampling: R0 = 0.999628.\nNotes: Layer i (counted from the substrate, as in the paper) has n_i = na + (-1)^(i+1) npa A(x_i), A = quintic of Eq. (2) with T = N lambda0/4 = 12.5 um, sampled at the layer centre x_i = (i - 1/2) lambda0/4. The sign follows sin(4 pi x/lambda0) of Eq. (1) with phi = 0, which is +1 at every odd layer's centre, so n_i equals n(x_i) of Eq. (1) with npp = 2 npa. The paper does not print the sampling point or the numeric indices; mid-layer sampling is an assumption. x = 0 is assumed at the substrate; the list is reversed so layer 1 here = layer N of the paper (next to the incident medium). Each layer is one QWOT at 1000 nm. Normal incidence; incident medium and substrate both n = na = 1.9 (n0 = ns = na), non-dispersive and lossless, so R is the same whichever side faces the light.\nLayers from Sec. 3, Eqs. (1)-(3), pp. 1261-1262; parameters of Figs. 1, 2(b), 2(c) (solid) and 3 (solid): N = 50, na = 1.9, npa = 0.35 (npp = 0.7), lambda0 = 1 um, n0 = ns = na; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R0 0.99962777 at 1000 nm; paper below the periodic stack of the same contrast (Fig. 3b). Each layer’s index is the paper’s wavelet (Eqs. 1-3) taken at the middle of the layer, which the paper does not specify.",
  "Wavelength": [
    1000
  ],
